(function(){var I=window.CLF_I18N=window.CLF_I18N||{};var L=I.en=I.en||{};['q','course','lessons','glossary','services','domains','ui'].forEach(function(k){L[k]=L[k]||{};});
Object.assign(L.q,{
"x1-01":{exp:"Keywords: deploying to a new geographic area in a very short time, close to users → Go global in minutes (thanks to the ready-made global infrastructure of Regions/AZs).",why:["Correct: The global infrastructure lets you bring an application close to customers on another continent in a short time.","Incorrect: Stop guessing capacity is about not having to buy too many or too few resources for a predicted load. It says nothing about geographic location.","Incorrect: Economies of scale is about lower prices because AWS aggregates the demand of a huge number of customers, not about the speed of entering a new market.","Incorrect: Trading CapEx for OpEx is about the payment model, while the scenario stresses the speed of reaching a new region."]},
"x1-02":{exp:"Agility = the time to get resources drops from weeks to minutes, and the cost of experimenting and failing is low → it is easy to experiment and innovate quickly.",why:["Correct: Getting resources in minutes with a low cost of failure is the definition of speed and agility.","Incorrect: High availability means keeping a system running continuously (for example, running in multiple AZs). It has nothing to do with how fast you can create test environments.","Incorrect: Fault tolerance means continuing to operate when a component fails, not the ability to experiment quickly.","Incorrect: Economies of scale explains why prices are low, not why the team can try ideas faster."]},
"x1-03":{exp:"AWS does not automatically copy data outside the Region you choose — the customer controls where data is stored (important for data residency). If you want a copy in another Region, you must configure it yourself (for example, S3 Cross-Region Replication).",why:["Correct (this is the inaccurate statement): Data stays in the chosen Region unless the customer actively configures replication to another Region.","Incorrect (because this statement is accurate): Each Region has multiple physically isolated AZs so that one incident does not affect all of them.","Incorrect (because this statement is accurate): AZs in the same Region are connected by high-bandwidth, low-latency networking, which allows synchronous replication.","Incorrect (because this statement is accurate): An AZ consists of one or more discrete data centers with redundant power, networking, and connectivity."]},
"x1-04":{exp:"Scalability: the system can grow to handle a larger load. Elasticity: it automatically scales with actual demand — out when load is high AND back in when load is low, so you pay only for what you use.",why:["Correct: The key difference is that elasticity scales in both directions with demand, including releasing resources when they are not needed.","Incorrect: The number of Regions or AZs is about geographic reach and availability, not the definition of scalability or elasticity.","Incorrect: It is the other way around — elasticity is usually associated with automatic scaling (for example, Auto Scaling).","Incorrect: Elasticity is exactly what helps reduce cost, because you release resources when load drops."]},
"x1-05":{exp:"The 'undifferentiated heavy lifting' (racking, power, cooling, replacing hardware) is handled by AWS → your staff can focus on customers and products.",why:["Correct: AWS takes care of running the physical data centers, so the IT team is freed up to do work that creates value.","Incorrect: Trading CapEx for OpEx is about how you pay. It does not directly free up time spent on hardware maintenance.","Incorrect: Stop guessing capacity solves the problem of buying too many or too few servers, not data center maintenance work.","Incorrect: Go global is about fast geographic expansion. It is unrelated to reducing infrastructure maintenance."]},
"x1-06":{exp:"Geographically distant DR → use another Region. Regions are isolated and far apart; you can create resources there when needed and pay as you go, without investing in a second data center.",why:["Correct: Regions are designed to be isolated and located in different geographic areas, which makes them suitable as a distant DR site.","Correct: No upfront investment is needed — you pay only for the DR resources you are using in the second Region.","Incorrect: AWS does not automatically create a standby copy in another Region; the customer must design and configure DR themselves (for example, with AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery).","Incorrect: Edge locations are used by CloudFront/Route 53 to cache and deliver content close to users. They do not run a production database.","Incorrect: The AZs in one Region are close to each other (in the same area) to keep latency low; if you need hundreds of kilometers of separation, use another Region."]},
"x1-07":{exp:"'Make frequent, small, reversible changes' is an Operational Excellence principle (along with safely automate where possible, refine operations frequently, anticipate failure, learn from all operational events and metrics...).",why:["Correct: Making small, frequent, reversible changes is a design principle of Operational Excellence.","Incorrect: Reliability has 'manage change through automation' and 'automatically recover from failure', but 'small, reversible changes' belongs to Operational Excellence.","Incorrect: Performance Efficiency has 'experiment more often', which is about trying out resource configurations, not the release process.","Incorrect: Security focuses on identity, traceability, and data protection, not on how changes are deployed."]},
"x1-08":{exp:"Use managed services to reduce operational complexity (Performance Efficiency): consume complex technologies (ML, NoSQL, media transcoding) as a service, so the team does not need to build deep expertise itself. Older documentation called this principle 'Democratize advanced technologies'.",why:["Correct: Using AI/ML as a managed service instead of building it yourself is a classic example of 'use managed services to reduce operational complexity' (old name: democratize advanced technologies).","Incorrect: 'implement granular cost allocation' (old name: analyze and attribute expenditure) means attributing costs in detail to each workload/owner. It is unrelated to using AI as a service.","Incorrect: Safely automate where possible (old name: perform operations as code) means automating infrastructure and operational procedures with code. It is unrelated to using AI services.","Incorrect: Automatically recover from failure (Reliability) means detecting failures and recovering automatically, not consuming ML technology as a service."]},
"x1-09":{exp:"'Keep people away from data' is a Security principle: reduce direct access to and manual handling of data to lower the risk of loss, modification, and human error.",why:["Correct: Using tools to reduce direct access to sensitive data is Security's 'keep people away from data'.","Incorrect: Operational Excellence has 'safely automate where possible' for operations, but the goal of protecting data from human access belongs to Security.","Incorrect: Reliability is about recovering from failure and meeting demand, not about limiting access to data.","Incorrect: Cost Optimization is about cost, not about controlling who can touch the data."]},
"x1-10":{exp:"Reliability has 5 principles: automatically recover from failure, test recovery procedures, scale horizontally, stop guessing capacity, manage change through automation. Note that 'stop guessing capacity' is also one of the 6 advantages of cloud computing, but in the Well-Architected Framework it belongs to Reliability.",why:["Correct: Both are design principles of Reliability — running out of capacity is a common cause of failure.","Incorrect: Performance Efficiency has use managed services to reduce operational complexity (formerly: democratize advanced technologies), deploy workloads closer to your customers (formerly: go global in minutes), build scalable architectures (formerly: use serverless architectures), experiment more often, consider mechanical sympathy, design for change and variable demand.","Incorrect: Operational Excellence has 'anticipate failure' (game days), but 'test recovery procedures' and 'stop guessing capacity' belong to Reliability.","Incorrect: Cost Optimization has implement Cloud Financial Management, adopt a consumption model, measure business outcomes relative to costs (formerly: measure overall efficiency)… but it does not include these two principles."]},
"x1-11":{exp:"Sustainability (current framework): understand your impact, establish sustainability goals, maximize utilization, anticipate and adopt new, more efficient hardware and software offerings, align computational approach to problem complexity, minimize the footprint of data and its processing. ('Use managed services' used to sit under Sustainability in older documentation. The current framework has it in three other pillars: Operational Excellence ('Use managed services'), Performance Efficiency ('Use managed services to reduce operational complexity'), and Cost Optimization ('Use managed services and automation…').) The distractors belong to Cost Optimization, Performance Efficiency, and Operational Excellence.",why:["Correct: Using new, more efficient hardware/software (for example, Graviton) reduces the energy used per unit of work.","Correct: Maximize utilization means not leaving resources running idle; consolidating workloads and rightsizing reduce wasted energy.","Incorrect: Cloud financial management is a Cost Optimization principle. Its goal is cost, not environmental impact.","Incorrect: Experiment more often is a Performance Efficiency principle.","Incorrect: Refine operations frequently is an Operational Excellence principle."]},
"x1-12":{exp:"AWS Well-Architected Tool: a free service in the console that reviews a workload through a set of questions organized by pillar, saves milestones, and produces an improvement plan with high/medium risks.",why:["Correct: Per-pillar question sets, milestones, and an improvement plan are features of the Well-Architected Tool.","Incorrect: Trusted Advisor automatically checks your running resources (cost, security, limits...) but has no architecture question set or milestones.","Incorrect: AWS Config conformance packs evaluate resource configurations against a set of compliance rules. They are not a Well-Architected architecture review.","Incorrect: Security Hub CSPM aggregates findings and runs security compliance checks, so it covers only the security aspect."]},
"x1-13":{exp:"'Analyze and attribute expenditure' (current name: Implement granular cost allocation) — pinpointing the exact cost of each system/team in order to measure ROI and optimize — is a Cost Optimization principle.",why:["Correct: Attributing costs to owners (cost allocation tags) is the 'analyze and attribute expenditure' principle (now: Implement granular cost allocation) of Cost Optimization.","Incorrect: Operational Excellence can use tags for operations, but the goal here is cost transparency and accountability.","Incorrect: Security uses tags for access control (ABAC), but the scenario is not about access permissions.","Incorrect: Sustainability measures environmental impact, not how the bill is split across teams."]},
"x1-14":{exp:"Operational Excellence: learn from all operational events and metrics, perform operations as code (now: Safely automate where possible), refine operations procedures frequently (now: Refine operations frequently), make frequent, small, reversible changes, anticipate failure. Encryption → Security; benchmarks → Performance Efficiency; Savings Plans → Cost Optimization.",why:["Correct: Learning from every incident and sharing the lessons is the 'learn from all operational events and metrics' principle.","Correct: Runbooks as code that are updated regularly combine 'perform operations as code' (now: Safely automate where possible) and 'refine operations procedures frequently' (now: Refine operations frequently).","Incorrect: Encrypting data at rest is Security's 'protect data in transit and at rest'.","Incorrect: Choosing resources based on benchmarks belongs to Performance Efficiency.","Incorrect: Savings Plans are a commitment-based discount, which belongs to Cost Optimization."]},
"x1-15":{exp:"No backups and restore never tested → Reliability (test recovery procedures). Sharing long-term access keys → Security (strong identity foundation, least privilege, traceability).",why:["Correct: The ability to recover from data loss and testing recovery procedures belong to Reliability.","Correct: Sharing long-term credentials violates Security's 'implement a strong identity foundation' and 'maintain traceability'.","Incorrect: Performance Efficiency is about selecting and using resources efficiently; no performance issue is mentioned.","Incorrect: Cost Optimization is about spending; both findings are about the risk of data loss and about access.","Incorrect: Sustainability is about environmental impact. It is unrelated to backups or credentials."]},
"x1-16":{exp:"Governance perspective: orchestrate your cloud initiatives while maximizing benefits and minimizing risks. Capabilities: program/project management, benefits management, risk management, cloud financial management, data governance. Stakeholders: CFO, CDO, CRO.",why:["Correct: Managing programs, benefits, risk, and cloud finances are Governance capabilities.","Incorrect: The Business perspective makes sure cloud investments advance the business strategy (strategy, portfolio, innovation, product management). It is not about controlling spending and risk.","Incorrect: The Operations perspective handles the day-to-day running of cloud services (monitoring, incidents, patching). It does not manage programs and finances.","Incorrect: The Security perspective handles security (IAM, threat detection, data protection), not benefits and cost management."]},
"x1-17":{exp:"Operations perspective: make sure cloud services meet the needs of the business. Capabilities: observability, event management, incident and problem management, change and release management, patch management, availability and continuity...",why:["Correct: Incidents, patching, observability, and change/release are capabilities of the Operations perspective.","Incorrect: The Platform perspective builds a scalable, hybrid cloud platform (architecture, CI/CD, provisioning). It is not about incident operations processes.","Incorrect: The Governance perspective manages programs, benefits, risk, and cost — not day-to-day operations.","Incorrect: The People perspective covers culture, organizational structure, and workforce skills."]},
"x1-18":{exp:"The 4 phases of the CAF: Envision (identify and prioritize opportunities) → Align (identify capability gaps and dependencies) → Launch (deliver pilots in production) → Scale (expand the pilots to the desired scale).",why:["Correct: Launch means putting pilots into production and demonstrating value incrementally.","Incorrect: Envision is the first phase — identifying and prioritizing transformation opportunities. The company has already passed this phase.","Incorrect: Align means identifying capability gaps and getting stakeholders aligned — the company has already done this.","Incorrect: Scale means expanding the successful pilots across the organization, which happens after Launch."]},
"x1-19":{exp:"The 4 business outcomes of the CAF: reduced business risk (reliability, security, fraud detection), improved ESG performance, increased revenue (new products, new markets), increased operational efficiency (lower operating costs, higher productivity).",why:["Correct: Better reliability, stronger security, and fraud detection all reduce business risk.","Incorrect: Increased revenue means creating new sources of income (new products, new markets). The scenario does not mention revenue.","Incorrect: ESG performance is about environmental, social, and governance matters (for example, cutting carbon), not fraud detection.","Incorrect: Operational efficiency means lower operating costs and higher productivity; the benefits listed mainly aim to reduce risk."]},
"x1-20":{exp:"Refactor/re-architect: change the architecture to use cloud-native features, usually driven by a new business need (scale, speed of delivering features). It takes the most effort but brings the biggest long-term benefit.",why:["Correct: Rewriting the application as microservices/serverless is an architecture change — refactor.","Incorrect: Replatform makes only small optimizations (for example, moving to RDS) without changing the core architecture.","Incorrect: Rehost keeps the application as is and moves it to EC2 — it does not solve the scaling and development-speed problems.","Incorrect: Relocate moves an entire virtualized environment (for example, VMware) to the cloud without modifying the applications."]},
"x1-21":{exp:"Relocate: move infrastructure at the hypervisor level (for example, VMware workloads to a VMware environment running on AWS) — no hardware purchases, no application changes, and the same operational tools. Rehost moves each server to EC2, so the VMware operational tools cannot be kept.",why:["Correct: Keeping the virtualization platform and operational processes while moving VMs in bulk at the hypervisor level is relocate.","Incorrect: Rehost moves each server to EC2; the applications do not change, but the environment and the VMware management tools do.","Incorrect: Replatform makes a few optimizations (such as switching to managed services), which goes against the requirement to change nothing.","Incorrect: Retain means keeping the workloads on premises, while the company wants to move to AWS."]},
"x1-22":{exp:"AWS Application Migration Service (MGN) is the primary service for rehosting: continuous block-level replication, automatic conversion to EC2, and a cutover with short downtime. It suits large numbers of servers.",why:["Correct: MGN automates lift and shift and replicates continuously until cutover, minimizing manual work.","Incorrect: VM Import/Export works for individual images, but it is manual and does not replicate continuously — a lot of effort for 300 servers.","Incorrect: Rebuilding with CloudFormation means building new infrastructure; you would still have to install and move the applications and data manually.","Incorrect: DMS migrates only databases, not entire servers (operating system, applications)."]},
"x1-23":{exp:"A migration between different engines (heterogeneous): SCT (or DMS Schema Conversion) converts the schema and code objects; DMS migrates the data and continuously replicates changes to keep downtime low.",why:["Correct: SCT converts the schema, views, and stored procedures from Oracle to PostgreSQL.","Correct: DMS migrates the data and can replicate continuously (CDC) to reduce downtime at cutover.","Incorrect: Application Migration Service rehosts whole servers onto EC2. It does not convert Oracle to Aurora PostgreSQL.","Incorrect: DataSync transfers files and objects (NFS, SMB, S3). It does not convert or migrate databases.","Incorrect: AWS Backup centrally manages backups. It does not convert database engines."]},
"x1-24":{exp:"DMS: the source keeps running during the migration; it supports homogeneous (Oracle→Oracle) and heterogeneous (Oracle→PostgreSQL, together with schema conversion) migrations, and change data capture (CDC) for ongoing replication. The source can be on premises, on EC2, or on RDS.",why:["Correct: This is the main strength of DMS — less downtime because the application keeps using the source database.","Correct: DMS supports both homogeneous and heterogeneous migrations.","Correct: CDC enables ongoing replication, used for a low-downtime cutover or to keep the target in sync long term.","Incorrect: DMS does not require stopping the source; the fact that the source keeps running is what reduces downtime.","Incorrect: The source can be on premises, on EC2, on RDS, or elsewhere.","Incorrect: Copying NFS/SMB file shares is the job of AWS DataSync, not DMS."]},
"x1-25":{exp:"People perspective: the bridge between technology and business. It covers culture, organizational structure, leadership, workforce transformation (retraining, hiring), and change management.",why:["Correct: Employees' fears, the skills to hire/train for, and change management all belong to the People perspective.","Incorrect: Platform covers the architecture and technical platform in the cloud.","Incorrect: Operations makes sure cloud services are delivered at the level the business needs.","Incorrect: Governance covers orchestrating programs, benefits, risk, and cloud finances."]},
"x1-26":{exp:"MAP: a comprehensive migration program that includes a methodology (assess, mobilize, migrate and modernize), tools, partners/Professional Services, and financial support (credits) to reduce migration costs. (Advanced — rarely appears on the real exam)",why:["Correct: MAP combines methodology, tools, expertise, and AWS investment for large migration projects.","Incorrect: AWS Activate supports startups with credits and resources. It is not a migration program for large enterprises.","Incorrect: AWS Marketplace is a catalog for finding and buying third-party software. It does not provide a migration methodology or funding.","Incorrect: Enterprise Support is a technical support plan (with a TAM for guidance and fast incident response), not a migration funding program."]},
"x1-27":{exp:"Variable expense: a cost that changes with actual usage (pay-as-you-go). Fixed expense/CapEx: bought upfront or committed long term, no matter how much or how little you use.",why:["Correct: Store more GB and you pay more, store fewer and you pay less — a variable cost.","Correct: On-Demand is billed for actual running time; stop the instance and you stop paying for compute.","Incorrect: Hardware bought upfront is a fixed capital expense (CapEx) that you pay whether you use it or not.","Incorrect: A long-term lease is a fixed cost that does not change with usage.","Incorrect: Perpetual licenses bought upfront are a fixed investment, not usage-based."]},
"x1-28":{exp:"With EC2 (IaaS), AWS bears the cost of the physical infrastructure (power, cooling, hardware, physical security). The customer still bears the costs of the 'in the cloud' part: patching the guest OS, licenses for software it brings, and managing applications — these must go into the TCO.",why:["Correct: Under shared responsibility, patching the guest OS on EC2 is the customer's job, so it still takes staff time.","Correct: Commercial software you install yourself (or BYOL) still needs licenses paid for by the customer.","Correct: Configuring and managing applications remains the customer's responsibility and cost.","Incorrect: Data center power and cooling are AWS's costs, already included in the service price.","Incorrect: AWS is responsible for replacing failed physical hardware.","Incorrect: Physical security of the data centers is AWS's responsibility."]},
"x1-29":{exp:"License Included: the service price already includes the software license (for example, RDS for SQL Server) — no need to buy or manage licenses separately, and you pay as you go. BYOL fits when you already own licenses.",why:["Correct: No existing licenses and no wish to manage licenses → License Included.","Incorrect: BYOL only pays off when the company already owns licenses; here it has none.","Incorrect: Buying perpetual licenses is an upfront cost and you still have to manage them yourself — the opposite of the requirement.","Incorrect: SQL Server Express has size and resource limits and is not a way to serve every reporting application; the question is about the licensing model."]},
"x1-30":{exp:"Dedicated Hosts: a dedicated physical server, visibility into sockets/cores, and host affinity — a good fit for BYOL with licenses counted per socket/core/VM. Dedicated Instances run on dedicated hardware but do not let you see or control the host.",why:["Correct: Dedicated Hosts show the physical sockets and cores and let you pin instances to a host, which satisfies the license terms.","Incorrect: Dedicated Instances run on hardware that is not shared, but they do not show sockets/cores and do not guarantee the same host.","Incorrect: Reserved Instances are a commitment-based discount model. They do not provide a dedicated physical server.","Incorrect: Spot Instances use cheap spare capacity and can be reclaimed. They give you no control over the physical server."]},
"x1-31":{exp:"On premises: you buy servers for the predicted peak load, and it is hard to shrink afterward. Cloud: you can measure usage and change the instance type/size at any time, so rightsizing is a continuous activity that reduces cost.",why:["Correct: The ability to change sizes based on actual usage data is why rightsizing is effective in the cloud.","Incorrect: AWS does not resize instances on its own; the customer must take action (possibly guided by recommendations such as AWS Compute Optimizer).","Incorrect: Prices differ by size; economies of scale make overall prices lower but do not make all sizes cost the same.","Incorrect: The price per vCPU usually scales linearly within an instance family; choosing too large a size only causes waste."]},
"x1-32":{exp:"The instances are needed only about 50 of 168 hours each week (~30%). Automating the stop outside working hours (for example, scheduling with Amazon EventBridge Scheduler or Instance Scheduler on AWS) cuts about 70% of the compute cost — better than committing to RIs for lightly used resources.",why:["Correct: You pay only for the hours actually used; automation makes sure nobody forgets to stop the instances.","Incorrect: RIs discount steady 24/7 usage; with usage around 30% of the time, a 3-year upfront commitment costs more than stopping the instances.","Incorrect: Dedicated Hosts are for licensing/compliance requirements, usually cost more, and do not reduce running hours.","Incorrect: Larger instances running all week still cost money during the hours when nobody uses them."]},
"x2-01":{exp:"With RDS (a managed service), AWS takes care of the operating system, installing and patching the database engine, and the hardware. The customer still takes care of what is inside the database and who can access it: DB users/privileges, security groups, encryption, and the data.",why:["Correct: Managing users and privileges inside the database is always the customer's job.","Correct: Configuring network access (security groups, subnets) is the customer's job.","Incorrect: With RDS, AWS patches the operating system of the DB host; the customer only chooses the maintenance window.","Incorrect: Physical hardware is always AWS's responsibility.","Incorrect: RDS installs the engine itself; the customer cannot access the underlying server."]},
"x2-02":{exp:"From EC2 (IaaS) to RDS (managed): responsibility for patching the OS and the database engine shifts to AWS. Network access, the encryption decision, and accounts inside the DB remain the customer's in both cases.",why:["Correct: On EC2 the customer patches the guest OS; on RDS AWS does it.","Incorrect: Network controls (security groups) are still configured by the customer on RDS.","Incorrect: Turning on encryption (KMS) is the customer's choice on both EC2 and RDS.","Incorrect: The accounts that log in to MySQL are still managed by the customer."]},
"x2-03":{exp:"With Lambda (serverless), AWS manages the infrastructure, the operating system, and the managed runtime (including patching). The customer is still responsible for the code, the libraries it packages, IAM permissions, and data.",why:["Correct: AWS patches the OS and the managed runtime for Lambda; on EC2 the customer has to do it.","Incorrect: Libraries in the deployment package are the customer's code, so the customer must update them.","Incorrect: The execution role and IAM permissions are always configured by the customer.","Incorrect: The security of the application code (input validation) is the customer's responsibility."]},
"x2-04":{exp:"S3 is an abstracted service: AWS takes care of the infrastructure, the OS, data durability, and destroying disks. The customer takes care of its own data and who can access it: bucket policies, ACLs, Block Public Access, and encryption choices.",why:["Correct: Configuring access to buckets/objects is the customer's job.","Incorrect: The customer never even sees the S3 servers; AWS patches them.","Incorrect: S3 Standard automatically stores redundant copies across multiple AZs; this is the part AWS handles.","Incorrect: Destroying storage devices is an AWS physical control."]},
"x2-05":{exp:"Inherited controls: the customer fully inherits them from AWS (physical, environmental). Shared controls: each side does its own part (patch management, configuration management, awareness & training). Customer-specific: the customer only (for example, data protection, network zoning).",why:["Correct: Physical/environmental controls are inherited controls.","Incorrect: Patch management is a shared control: AWS patches the infrastructure, and the customer patches the guest OS and applications.","Incorrect: Configuration management is a shared control: AWS configures the infrastructure, and the customer configures its OS, databases, and applications.","Incorrect: Awareness & training is a shared control: AWS trains AWS employees, and the customer trains its own staff."]},
"x2-06":{exp:"SSE-KMS: every time S3 uses the KMS key to encrypt/decrypt, an event is generated in CloudTrail → key usage can be audited. Enforcing encryption in transit → a bucket policy with Deny when aws:SecureTransport = false.",why:["Correct: With KMS keys, every use of the key is recorded in CloudTrail for auditing.","Correct: The aws:SecureTransport condition blocks every unencrypted HTTP request.","Incorrect: Transfer Acceleration speeds up uploads through edge locations. It does not enforce TLS or log key usage.","Incorrect: Versioning keeps multiple versions of an object. It is unrelated to encryption.","Incorrect: Object Lock prevents deletion/overwriting (WORM). It does not log key usage or enforce TLS."]},
"x2-07":{exp:"Event history keeps only 90 days of management events, and that cannot be changed. For longer retention: create a trail that delivers logs to S3 (you can add a lifecycle rule to S3 Glacier to reduce cost). Config stores configuration history, not API history; Artifact holds AWS's compliance reports.",why:["Correct: A trail delivers log files to S3, where you can keep them as long as you like.","Incorrect: Event history is fixed at 90 days and cannot be extended.","Incorrect: Config records changes to resource configurations, not every API call.","Incorrect: Artifact provides AWS's compliance reports. It does not store your account activity."]},
"x2-08":{exp:"AWS Artifact has two parts: Reports (download SOC, PCI, ISO… reports) and Agreements (review and accept agreements such as the BAA). Audit Manager, Security Hub, and Config help assess your own resources; they do not sign legal agreements with AWS.",why:["Correct: The BAA is accepted through Artifact Agreements.","Incorrect: Audit Manager collects evidence for your own audits. It is not where you sign agreements with AWS.","Incorrect: Security Hub checks configurations against security standards. It does not handle agreements.","Incorrect: A conformance pack is just a set of Config rules for evaluating resources."]},
"x2-09":{exp:"Every API call to KMS (Encrypt, Decrypt, GenerateDataKey…) is recorded in CloudTrail, along with the caller's identity and the time. “Who used the key, when” → CloudTrail.",why:["Incorrect: CloudWatch metrics measure counts and performance; they do not record who made each request.","Correct: CloudTrail records every API call to KMS, including the principal, the time, and the source address.","Incorrect: Config records the configuration of resources (for example, the key policy), not each time a key is used.","Incorrect: Macie finds sensitive data in S3. It does not record the use of KMS keys."]},
"x2-10":{exp:"Compliance also follows shared responsibility: AWS certifies the infrastructure part (security OF the cloud), and the customer handles the workload part (IN the cloud). The customer chooses the Region, and AWS does not move content out of the chosen Region on its own.",why:["Correct: AWS certifications do not automatically apply to the customer's applications.","Correct: The customer controls the Region where its data is stored.","Incorrect: HIPAA-eligible only means the service can be used for PHI under a BAA; the customer must still configure it correctly.","Incorrect: The customer's application must be audited separately; AWS does not issue certificates for it.","Incorrect: AWS does not move content out of the Region the customer chose unless the customer asks for it."]},
"x2-11":{exp:"Finding software vulnerabilities (CVEs) in EC2, ECR container images, and Lambda functions → Amazon Inspector. GuardDuty detects active threats, Detective investigates, and Macie finds sensitive data.",why:["Correct: Inspector continuously scans EC2, ECR images, and Lambda for software vulnerabilities.","Incorrect: GuardDuty analyzes logs to detect malicious activity. It does not inventory vulnerable packages.","Incorrect: Detective helps investigate the root cause of a security finding. It does not scan for vulnerabilities.","Incorrect: Macie finds sensitive data (PII) in S3."]},
"x2-12":{exp:"Security Hub = configuration checks against standards (CIS, AWS Foundational Security Best Practices, PCI DSS) + collecting findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, and third-party products in one place, across many accounts. Note: since 2025, the standards-based security posture checks (CIS, AWS Foundational Security Best Practices…) are named AWS Security Hub CSPM; the CLF-C02 exam guide still calls it AWS Security Hub.",why:["Correct: Security Hub both runs security standard checks and aggregates findings.","Incorrect: Detective is for investigating a finding in depth. It does not run CIS checks.","Incorrect: Firewall Manager centrally applies firewall rules. It does not collect findings from Macie/Inspector.","Incorrect: Audit Manager collects evidence for audits; it is not the place to gather and handle day-to-day security findings."]},
"x2-13":{exp:"Detecting active threats (crypto mining, communication with malicious IPs/domains, exposed credentials) from CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs, with no agents needed → GuardDuty.",why:["Correct: GuardDuty analyzes DNS logs and Flow Logs to detect crypto mining.","Incorrect: Inspector finds software vulnerabilities. It does not watch for malicious network communication.","Incorrect: Config tracks configuration changes. It does not detect threats.","Incorrect: Shield Standard protects your resources against DDoS. It does not detect a compromised instance."]},
"x2-14":{exp:"Three services that are often confused: CloudWatch = metrics, logs, alarms (performance, operations). CloudTrail = who called which API, and when. Config = how resource configurations changed over time and whether they comply with rules.",why:["Correct: CloudWatch Logs + a metric filter + an alarm for the error logs.","Correct: CloudTrail records which user called the API that modified the security group.","Correct: Config stores configuration history and evaluates it with Config rules.","Incorrect: Detective is for investigating security findings. It does not replace these three foundational services.","Incorrect: Inspector scans for vulnerabilities. It does not monitor logs or configurations.","Incorrect: Trusted Advisor gives best-practice recommendations. It does not store configuration history or who did what."]},
"x2-15":{exp:"IAM provides 'last accessed information' for users, groups, roles, and policies: which services were used and when they were last used → use it to refine permissions toward least privilege. IAM Access Analyzer can also generate a policy from actual activity.",why:["Correct: Last accessed information is available right in IAM, so it takes the least effort.","Incorrect: The credential report lists users with the status of their passwords, access keys, and MFA; it does not include roles and does not show which services were used.","Incorrect: Querying CloudTrail would work but takes far more effort.","Incorrect: Trusted Advisor checks general best practices. It does not list the services a role has used."]},
"x2-16":{exp:"IAM Access Analyzer analyzes resource-based policies to find resources with external access (S3, KMS keys, IAM roles, Lambda, SQS, Secrets Manager…), and it also finds unused access.",why:["Correct: Access Analyzer generates findings for resources shared outside your zone of trust.","Incorrect: GuardDuty detects malicious activity. It does not analyze resource-sharing policies.","Incorrect: Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and network reachability.","Incorrect: The credential report only covers the sign-in credentials of IAM users."]},
"x2-17":{exp:"Some tasks only the root user can do: changing account settings (email, root password…), closing the account, restoring IAM permissions when the only administrator locks themselves out, activating IAM access to Billing, enabling MFA delete on S3, editing/deleting a bucket policy (or SQS policy) that denies all principals, and registering as a seller in the RI Marketplace. Changing the Support plan is NO LONGER a root-only task.",why:["Correct: MFA delete can be enabled only with root user credentials.","Correct: Activating IAM access to Billing and Cost Management is a root-only task.","Correct: Only the root user can edit/delete a bucket policy that denies all principals.","Incorrect: According to current documentation, an IAM principal with the right permissions can change the Support plan.","Incorrect: An IAM user/role with IAM permissions can create other users.","Incorrect: AWS Budgets can be used by an IAM user with the appropriate permissions."]},
"x2-18":{exp:"Centralized root access (IAM + Organizations) lets you remove the root password, access keys, and MFA from member accounts and run short-lived privileged root tasks from the management account. An SCP only restricts actions; it does not remove credentials. (Advanced — rarely appears on the real exam)",why:["Correct: This is exactly the purpose of centralized root access management.","Incorrect: An SCP can restrict the root user of member accounts, but it does not remove root credentials and does not provide a central way to perform root tasks.","Incorrect: Permission sets grant access to workforce users. They do not manage the root user.","Incorrect: The password policy does not apply to the root user's password."]},
"x2-19":{exp:"IAM supports three types of MFA: passkeys/FIDO security keys, virtual authenticator apps, and hardware TOTP tokens. Only FIDO (passkeys/security keys) is phishing-resistant because it is bound to the real domain; users can still be tricked into typing a TOTP code into a fake page. SMS is no longer supported for IAM.",why:["Correct: FIDO2/passkeys are bound to the correct website, so they resist phishing.","Incorrect: A user can still type a TOTP code from an app into a spoofed page.","Incorrect: A hardware TOTP token also relies on six-digit codes, so it is not phishing-resistant.","Incorrect: IAM does not support MFA over SMS."]},
"x2-20":{exp:"The IAM password policy applies to IAM users: length, character types, expiration, and reuse prevention. It does not apply to the root user's password.",why:["Correct (it CANNOT do this): The password policy does not apply to the root user.","Incorrect (it can do this): Setting a minimum length is a basic feature of the password policy.","Incorrect (it can do this): You can make passwords expire after N days.","Incorrect (it can do this): You can block the reuse of a number of recent passwords."]},
"x2-21":{exp:"Best practice: humans use federation/IAM Identity Center to get temporary credentials; avoid long-term access keys. Rotating keys is better than not rotating them, but they are still long-term credentials.",why:["Correct: Short-term credentials expire on their own, so no long-term keys are left on the laptop.","Incorrect: These are still long-term credentials; rotation only reduces the risk, it does not remove it.","Incorrect: Sharing keys destroys the ability to trace actions to an individual, and they are still long-term keys.","Incorrect: Storing keys in the repository (even encrypted) increases the risk of exposure, and they are still long-term keys."]},
"x2-22":{exp:"Parameter Store (standard parameters) stores configuration values and SecureString values (encrypted with KMS) at no additional storage charge. Secrets Manager charges per secret and is worth the money when you need automatic rotation.",why:["Correct: Parameter Store fits configuration + encrypted values with no rotation needed, at low cost.","Incorrect: Secrets Manager would work but charges per secret; its main advantage is automatic rotation, which the question does not need.","Incorrect: CloudHSM is a dedicated HSM for managing keys. It is expensive and not meant for storing configuration.","Incorrect: ACM manages SSL/TLS certificates. It does not store configuration values."]},
"x2-23":{exp:"The two standard places to store secrets: Secrets Manager (with automatic rotation) and Parameter Store SecureString (encrypted with KMS). User data, tags, and default values in a template can all be seen in plaintext by anyone allowed to read the metadata/template.",why:["Correct: Secrets Manager stores, encrypts, and can rotate secrets.","Correct: A SecureString is encrypted with KMS and controlled with IAM.","Incorrect: User data is not encrypted, and anyone allowed to view the instance attributes can read it.","Incorrect: Tags are plaintext metadata and are not meant for secrets.","Incorrect: Templates are usually kept in a repository; a default value is exposed just like a hardcoded one."]},
"x2-24":{exp:"Workforce (employees) accessing multiple AWS accounts → IAM Identity Center. Customers (app users) signing up and signing in → Cognito. Directory Service is managed Microsoft AD, not a sign-up solution for app customers.",why:["Correct: IAM Identity Center provides SSO for employees into multiple accounts.","Correct: A Cognito user pool lets the app's customers sign up/sign in.","Incorrect: Directory Service provides Active Directory; it can be an identity source, but by itself it does not provide multi-account SSO or app sign-up.","Incorrect: RAM shares resources between accounts. It does not manage identities.","Incorrect: Secrets Manager stores secrets. It does not authenticate users."]},
"x2-25":{exp:"Access from another account (including a third party) → a cross-account IAM role; the other side assumes the role to get temporary credentials. An external ID helps avoid the 'confused deputy' problem when granting access to a third party.",why:["Correct: A cross-account role + external ID + read-only permissions, with no long-term keys.","Incorrect: Long-term keys created for outsiders are hard to control and hard to revoke at the right time.","Incorrect: RAM shares certain resource types (subnets, Transit Gateway…). It does not grant read access to the whole account.","Incorrect: An SCP does not grant permissions; and bringing an external account into the organization is not appropriate."]},
"x2-26":{exp:"A group is just a collection of users for attaching shared policies: it cannot be nested, cannot contain roles, and is not a principal (it cannot sign in and cannot be listed in Principal). A role is assumed to receive temporary credentials.",why:["Correct: A group contains only users, not other groups.","Correct: A role has no password/long-term access keys; assuming a role returns temporary credentials.","Incorrect: A group contains only users; a role cannot be a member of a group.","Incorrect: IAM does not support nested groups.","Incorrect: A group is not a principal, so it cannot be listed in the Principal element of a resource policy."]},
"x2-27":{exp:"Policy evaluation rule: deny by default → an allow permits the request → but an explicit deny in any policy always wins. There is no priority order between a user's policies and a group's policies.",why:["Correct: The explicit deny wins over the allow from AdministratorAccess.","Incorrect: No matter how broad an allow is, an explicit deny overrides it.","Incorrect: There is no such thing as group policies taking priority; all policies are evaluated together.","Incorrect: The deny here has no MFA condition; it applies in every case."]},
"x2-28":{exp:"AWS managed policies (including job-function policies such as SecurityAudit and ViewOnlyAccess) are written by AWS and updated when new services arrive → the least effort. Customer managed/inline policies must be written and maintained by you. An SCP does not grant permissions.",why:["Correct: An AWS managed policy is maintained by AWS; attach it to the group once and you are done.","Incorrect: This would work, but you must update it yourself every time AWS adds a service, which takes effort.","Incorrect: Inline policies for each person are hard to manage, and you still have to maintain them yourself.","Incorrect: An SCP only sets the maximum boundary. It does not grant permissions to anyone."]},
"x2-29":{exp:"A WAF rate-based rule automatically blocks IPs that exceed a request threshold within a time window, which fits brute force/bots at Layer 7. A network ACL must be edited by hand and has a limit on the number of rules. GuardDuty only detects; it does not block.",why:["Correct: A WAF rate-based rule automatically blocks IPs that send too many requests.","Incorrect: A NACL needs manual updates for each IP and cannot keep up with constantly changing IPs.","Incorrect: GuardDuty detects threats. It does not block HTTP requests by itself.","Incorrect: Shield Standard protects against Layer 3/4 DDoS. It does not handle password guessing at the application layer."]},
"x2-30":{exp:"A security group can use another security group as its source. Every instance attached to the web tier's security group is allowed automatically, including new instances created by Auto Scaling, so there are no IPs to manage.",why:["Correct: Referencing a security group as the source automatically covers every current and future instance of the web tier.","Incorrect: The IPs change constantly, so you would be editing rules forever; a NACL is also stateless and applies to the whole subnet, which is more complex.","Incorrect: AWS WAF protects web applications (CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway…). It does not filter MySQL connections between EC2 instances.","Incorrect: An IAM policy controls AWS API calls, not network connections to port 3306 of a self-installed MySQL."]},
"x2-31":{exp:"The web application protection trio: WAF (SQLi, XSS, Layer 7), Shield Advanced (advanced DDoS protection + SRT + cost protection), Firewall Manager (centrally applies WAF/Shield Advanced/security groups across the whole organization, including new accounts).",why:["Correct: WAF blocks SQL injection.","Correct: Shield Advanced includes access to the Shield Response Team.","Correct: Firewall Manager automatically applies rules to new accounts/resources in the organization.","Incorrect: Macie finds sensitive data in S3.","Incorrect: Inspector finds software vulnerabilities. It does not block attacks.","Incorrect: Detective investigates incidents. It does not block attacks."]},
"x2-32":{exp:"Finding sensitive data (PII, card numbers, passport numbers…) in S3 using machine learning and pattern matching → Amazon Macie.",why:["Correct: Macie discovers sensitive data in S3 objects.","Incorrect: GuardDuty detects suspicious behavior (including on S3). It does not classify object contents.","Incorrect: Config tracks bucket configuration. It does not read the data itself.","Incorrect: Access Analyzer tells you whether a bucket is shared externally, not what is inside it."]},
"x2-33":{exp:"GuardDuty detects → Detective investigates: it automatically builds a behavior graph from CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and GuardDuty findings to visualize relationships and find the root cause.",why:["Correct: Detective is built for investigating and visualizing the scope of an incident.","Incorrect: Security Hub collects and prioritizes findings. It is not a detailed behavior analysis tool.","Incorrect: Config shows how configurations changed. It does not analyze the activity of a role or an IP address.","Incorrect: Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities."]},
"x2-34":{exp:"KMS customer managed keys: you control the key policy, enabling/disabling, and automatic rotation; every use of the key is logged to CloudTrail; and they integrate out of the box with most AWS services. CloudHSM also gives you control, but you manage the HSM cluster yourself, which takes more effort.",why:["Correct: KMS customer managed keys meet every requirement and are operated by AWS.","Incorrect: CloudHSM is for when you need a single-tenant HSM; it takes far more operational effort.","Incorrect: Secrets Manager stores secrets such as passwords. It is not an encryption key management system for services.","Incorrect: ACM private certificates are for TLS. They are not used to encrypt data at rest in services."]},
"x2-35":{exp:"ACM issues public certificates for integrated services (ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway…) and renews them automatically when they are validated through DNS. Certificates uploaded to IAM or imported must be renewed by you.",why:["Correct: ACM automatically renews the certificates it issues for ALB and CloudFront.","Incorrect: IAM server certificates are certificates you bring yourself; IAM does not renew them automatically.","Incorrect: Secrets Manager can store certificate files, but it does not issue or renew TLS certificates.","Incorrect: KMS key rotation has nothing to do with the website's TLS certificates."]},
"x2-36":{exp:"The Knowledge Center (on re:Post) contains articles written by AWS that answer common questions and errors with step-by-step guidance. The Security Blog publishes posts about new security features and best practices; Artifact holds compliance reports.",why:["Correct: The Knowledge Center is for common questions/errors with troubleshooting guidance.","Incorrect: The Security Blog has in-depth posts, but it is not a library of troubleshooting guides for common errors.","Incorrect: Artifact only provides compliance reports and agreements.","Incorrect: Trusted Advisor checks your account against best practices. It does not give troubleshooting steps for a specific error."]},
"x2-37":{exp:"Trusted Advisor has a set of core checks that are free on every Support plan, including security checks such as security groups with open ports, MFA on the root user, and S3 bucket permissions. Higher plans unlock all checks.",why:["Correct: Trusted Advisor's core security checks are available with Basic Support.","Incorrect: Security Hub can run similar checks, but it is a paid service.","Incorrect: GuardDuty detects threats and charges based on the volume of data analyzed.","Incorrect: Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities, is a paid service, and does not check root user MFA."]},
"x2-38":{exp:"Since early 2023, S3 automatically encrypts every new object with SSE-S3 as the default level. If you want to use your own KMS key, configure SSE-KMS. HTTPS is encryption in transit; it does not determine encryption at rest.",why:["Correct: SSE-S3 is the default base level of encryption for every bucket.","Incorrect: No new object is stored unencrypted anymore.","Incorrect: SSE-KMS is used only when you configure it; the default is SSE-S3.","Incorrect: HTTPS protects data in transit. It is unrelated to encryption at rest."]}
});
})();
